The question asks whether the body of an arrow function (fn) in PHP may consist of several expressions in curly braces.

In PHP, arrow functions (fn) introduced in PHP 7.4 have a specific syntax. The body of an arrow function must be a single expression followed by a semicolon. It does NOT use curly braces. The syntax is:

fn($param) => expression;

If you need multiple statements, you use a regular anonymous function (closure) with curly braces:

function($param) { ... }

So the statement "The body of an arrow function fn may consist of several expressions in curly braces" is FALSE. Arrow functions have exactly one expression as their body, and they don't use curly braces.

The code is:
$x = 5;
$f = function () use ($x) { return $x; };
$x = 7;
echo $f();

In PHP, when you use `use ($x)` in a closure, the variable is captured by value at the time the closure is defined. So `$x` is captured as 5. Even though `$x` is later changed to 7, the closure still has the original value of 5.

So the output will be 5.

PHP constructor property promotion was introduced in PHP 8.0. It allows you to declare class properties directly in the constructor parameters. The syntax is like:

public function __construct(private int $x) {}

