The question asks whether a private parent method is not part of the inheritance contract, meaning a child class can declare a method with the same name and any visibility.

Let me think about this in PHP:

In PHP, private methods are not inherited. They are not part of the inheritance contract. A child class can indeed declare a method with the same name as a private method in the parent class, and it can have any visibility (public, protected, private). The child's method is completely independent of the parent's private method.

For example:
```php
class Parent {
    private function foo() {
        // parent implementation
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    public function foo() {
        // child implementation - this is allowed
    }
}
```

This is valid PHP. The private method in the parent is essentially invisible to the child class. The child can define its own method with the same name and any visibility.

So the statement is TRUE.

The question asks whether the instanceof operator in PHP only checks class inheritance and ignores interfaces.

In PHP, the `instanceof` operator checks if an object is an instance of a specific class OR if it implements a specific interface. So it does NOT ignore interfaces. For example:

```php
interface MyInterface {}
class MyClass implements MyInterface {}
$obj = new MyClass();
var_dump($obj instanceof MyInterface); // true
```

So the statement "it ignores interfaces" is false. The instanceof operator checks both class inheritance AND interface implementation.

The answer is "no" (false).

